---
import GuideArticlePage from '../../components/templates/GuideArticlePage.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How do I know if my watch is worth repairing?',
    a: 'Compare a written repair estimate to realistic market value for your exact reference and condition, then adjust for sentimental weight — the math is only half the story.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What watches are generally not worth repairing?',
    a: 'Low-value quartz with module failure, heavily damaged fashion pieces, and some mass-market models where parts exceed replacement cost often fail the ratio test — but exceptions exist when the watch is irreplaceable personally.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much should I expect to pay for watch repair?',
    a: 'Common starting points: battery work from roughly $25, crystals from roughly $80, and mechanical overhauls from roughly $250 — with final quotes dependent on parts and movement complexity.',
  },
];
---

<GuideArticlePage
  title="Is My Watch Worth Repairing? | Shelton Watch Services"
  description="A practical framework for deciding if watch repair makes sense — cost vs. value, sentimental weight, and when to ask a watchmaker."
  h1="Is My Watch Worth Repairing?"
  answer="If honest repair costs approach a large fraction of what the watch could reasonably sell for — and the piece is not emotionally irreplaceable — selling often wins. A common rule of thumb starts evaluating tension around roughly half of market value: below that, repair frequently makes sense; above it, pause and compare options."
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHref="/tools/repair-or-sell/"
  ctaLabel="Try the repair-or-sell tool →"
>
  <p>
    “Worth it” is two questions disguised as one: financial worth and personal worth. On the financial
    side, collectors often anchor to a <strong>40–60%</strong> repair-to-value band before skepticism kicks
    in — but a rare dial or family engraving can blow that model up instantly.
  </p>
  <h2>How Should You Compare Repair Cost to Market Value?</h2>
  <p>
    Market value means <em>your</em> reference in <em>your</em> condition — not a cherry-picked auction
    highlight. Start with comparable sales that match bracelet type, service history signals, and dial
    integrity. If you cannot find comps, assume wider uncertainty and lean on a professional opinion.
  </p>
  <h2>When Does Sentimental Value Change the Math?</h2>
  <p>
    If losing the watch would bother you more than losing the cash, repair is often the path — just do it
    with eyes open about diminishing returns. Sentimental pieces still deserve written estimates so you are
    choosing consciously, not drifting into a money pit.
  </p>
  <h2>What Is the Fastest Way to Stop Guessing?</h2>
  <p>
    A short appointment with documented condition notes beats weeks of forum scrolling. If you are in the
    Lehigh Valley, book an evaluation — or use our{' '}
    <a href="/tools/repair-or-sell/">repair-or-sell tool</a> for a first-pass nudge, then confirm with a
    human. For documentation needs, see <a href="/service/watch-appraisal/">watch appraisal</a>.
  </p>
</GuideArticlePage>
